WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
The field of
information retrieval has

2
00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.880
been around for a while as
a subfield of

3
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.580
natural language processing,
and there's many approaches to

4
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.700
improving the relevancy of query results.
But what's new is we have powerful

5
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.080
large language models, and we
can use those to augment and enhance the

6
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.260
queries that we send
to our vector-based retrieval system

7
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.260
to get better
results.

8
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:18.500
Let's take a look.

9
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.760
So the first type of query expansion we're
going to talk about is called

10
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.400
expansion with generated
answers.

11
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.140
Typically the way that this works
is you take your query and

12
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.000
you pass it over to an
LLM which you prompt to generate

13
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.480
a hypothetical or
imagined answer to your query and

14
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.280
then you concatenate
your query with the imagined answer

15
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.200
and use that as the new
query which you pass to your

16
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.880
retrieval system or vector database.

17
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.760
Then you return your
query results as normal. Let's

18
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.560
take a look at how
this works in practice.

19
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.160
So the first thing that we're
going to do is once

20
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.380
again and grab all the
utilities that we need.

21
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.080
And we're going to load
everything we need from Chrome and

22
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.220
create our embedding function.

23
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.870
And we're going to set up
our OpenAI client again, because

24
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.590
we'll be using the LLM.

25
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.310
And once again,
to help with visualization, we're

26
00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:14.550
going to use UMAP and project
our data set so that that's all

27
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.650
ready to go for us.

28
00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.310
Now that we're done setting up, let's take
a look at expansion with

29
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.790
generated answers and there's a
reference here to the paper which demonstrates

30
00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.510
some of the
empirical results that you can get

31
00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:35.930
by applying this method.

32
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.850
So to do expansion
with generated answers we're

33
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.270
going to use an LLM, in
this case GPT, and

34
00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.530
just the same as last time we're going
to prompt the model in

35
00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.470
a particular way.

36
00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.250
Let's create this function
called augment query

37
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.930
generated and we're going
to pass in a query, we're also going

38
00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.070
to pass in a model argument, in this
case GPT 3.5 turbo by default,

39
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.510
and we're going to prompt the
model and in the system prompt

40
00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.370
we're going to say you're a
helpful expert financial research assistant, provide an example

41
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.810
answer to the given
question that might be found in

42
00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.430
a document, like an annual report.

43
00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.390
In other words, we're
pretty much asking the

44
00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.950
model to hallucinate, but we're gonna
use that hallucination for

45
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:08.730
something useful.

46
00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:12.470
And in the user prompt, we're
just gonna pass the query as the content.

47
00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.430
And then we'll do
our usual unpacking of

48
00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:14.970
the response.

49
00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.310
And that defines how we're
going to prompt our model.

50
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:21.850
Let's wire this together.

51
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.850
Here's our original query, asking
was there a

52
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.750
significant turnover in
the executive team.

53
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.910
We will generate a
hypothetical answer and then we'll

54
00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.850
create our joint query which is
basically the original

55
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.950
query prepending the
hypothetical answer.

56
00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:37.990
Now let's take a look at
what this actually looks

57
00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.190
like after we generate it.

58
00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.850
So here we see the output.
We see our original query,

59
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.750
was there a significant
turnover in the executive team,

60
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.070
and a hypothetical answer.

61
00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:46.550
In the past fiscal
year there was no

62
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.790
significant turnover in the
executive team, the core members of

63
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:50.750
the executive team remained
unchanged, etc.

64
00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.990
So let's send this query plus
the hypothetical critical response

65
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:57.070
to our retrieval system
as a query.

66
00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.950
And we'll query the Chroma Collection
the usual way and

67
00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:01.870
print out our results.

68
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.430
And we're sending the joint query
as the query to

69
00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.750
our retrieval system. And we're retrieving
the documents and the

70
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.830
embeddings together. So these
are the documents that we get.

71
00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.070
Back, we see things
here discussing leadership.

72
00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.390
We see how consultants
and directors work together.

73
00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:16.290
Here we have an
overview of the different

74
00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.210
directors that we had in Microsoft.

75
00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.850
And we talk about
the different board committees. Let's

76
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.970
visualize this. Let's see
what sort of difference this made.

77
00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.690
So, to do that,
we get our retrieved embeddings,

78
00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.710
we get the embedding
for our original query,

79
00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.510
we get the embedding
for our joint query,

80
00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.310
and then we project all three.

81
00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:32.610
I'm plotting the projection.

82
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.220
And we see the red
X is our original query,

83
00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.920
the orange X is our
new query with the hypothetical

84
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.820
answer. And we see that we
get this nice cluster of results,

85
00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.440
but most importantly, what I want
to illustrate here is that using

86
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.760
the hypothetical answer
moves our query elsewhere in space,

87
00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.820
hopefully producing better
results for us.

88
00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.300
So that was query
expansion with generated queries,

89
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.340
but there's another type of query expansion
we can also try. This

90
00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.780
is called query expansion
with multiple queries. And

91
00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.740
the way that you use this is to
use the LLM to

92
00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.940
generate additional queries that
might help answering the

93
00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.760
question. So what you do here is
you take your original query, you

94
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.820
pass it to the LLM, you ask the. LLM
to generate several new

95
00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:14.120
related queries to the
same original query, and

96
00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.940
then you pass those
new queries along with

97
00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.160
your original query to
the vector database or

98
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.340
your retrieval system. That
gives you results for the original

99
00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.520
and the new queries, and
then you pass all of

100
00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.400
those results to the LLM
to complete the RAG loop.

101
00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.840
So let's take a look at how
this works in practice.

102
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.480
Once again, the starting point is
a prompt in the model,

103
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.620
and we see here that
we have a system prompt,

104
00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.060
and the system prompt is
a bit more detailed this time.

105
00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.420
We take in a query,
which is our original query,

106
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.840
and we ask the model. It's
a helpful expert

107
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.420
financial research assistant. The
users are asking questions

108
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.920
about an annual report, so
this gives the model enough context

109
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:46.920
to know what sorts of queries
to generate. And

110
00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.080
then you say, suggest up to five
additional related questions

111
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.720
to help them find the information
they need for

112
00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:55.600
the provided question. Suggest
only short questions

113
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.840
without compound sentences,
and this makes sure that

114
00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.060
we get simple queries.

115
00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.420
Suggest a variety of
questions that cover different

116
00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.720
aspects of the topic, and this is
very important because

117
00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.560
there are many ways to rephrase
the same query, but what we're

118
00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.100
actually asking for is
different but related queries.

119
00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.820
And finally,
we want to make sure that they're

120
00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:14.500
complete questions, they're related
to the original question, and we

121
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.600
ask some formatting output.

122
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.320
One important thing
to understand about

123
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.200
these techniques in
particular that bring an LLM into

124
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.120
the loop of retrieval is prompt
engineering becomes a concern. It's

125
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.280
something that you have
to think about and

126
00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.880
I really recommend that
you as a student

127
00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.660
play with these prompts
once you have a

128
00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:30.840
chance to try the lab.

129
00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.520
See how they may change,
see what different types

130
00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.120
of queries you can
get the models to generate,

131
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.200
try different models,
and basically experiment,

132
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.740
not just with the retrieval system,
but with the prompts you're

133
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.360
using to augment your queries.

134
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.520
So let's
define this function and let's see

135
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.800
what we get when we
actually try this. So here's our original

136
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:47.260
query.

137
00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.960
What were the most
important factors that contributed

138
00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.420
to increases in revenue?

139
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.840
So this is a compound query.

140
00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.960
There could be many,
many different factors and it's

141
00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:58.020
not just about revenue.

142
00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.080
And let's see what augmented queries
we get back.

143
00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:04.760
We'll ask the model and let's print
a set of augmented

144
00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.080
queries as output.

145
00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.460
Great, so we got a few back.
We see that what were the

146
00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.080
most important factors
that contributed to

147
00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.860
decreases in revenue?
Great question.

148
00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.180
What were the sources of revenue?
Also very important.

149
00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.900
How were sales
and revenue distributed

150
00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.660
across the different product lines?
Were there any changes in pricing

151
00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:19.340
strategy?

152
00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.900
Did the company acquire any new
customers? So you can see

153
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.360
that these are related
questions to our original query,

154
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.440
but they're not precisely the same and
they have different meanings. That's

155
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.080
very, very useful, and that's a great
illustration of augmenting

156
00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.940
an original query
through query expansion

157
00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.800
with multiple queries.
So let's see how this

158
00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.720
works in practice once
we pass these queries

159
00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:36.800
to our retrieval system.

160
00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:39.180
So first,
we build our set of queries. Now,

161
00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.000
Chroma can handle
multiple queries in parallel.

162
00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.900
So what we're doing here is taking
our original query in

163
00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.100
an array and then
concatenating that with our

164
00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:49.320
array of augmented queries. So
now we have one array where

165
00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.720
each entry is a query, our
original query, plus

166
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:52.780
the augmented queries.

167
00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.000
And we can grab the results.

168
00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.980
And again,
Chroma can do querying in batches.

169
00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.000
And let's look at the retrieved
documents that we get.

170
00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.440
And one thing that's
important here is because

171
00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.560
the queries are related, you
might get the same

172
00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.560
document retrieved for more
than one query. So what we need to

173
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.120
do is to deduplicate
the retrieved and that's what

174
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:11.240
we do here. And finally, let's just output
the documents that we get.

175
00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.950
So we can see now the
documents that we get for each

176
00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:17.370
query.

177
00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:20.190
And these are all to do with revenue,
different aspects of revenue growth,

178
00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.810
which is exactly what
we were hoping for.

179
00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.770
We have increases in Windows revenue.

180
00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.990
We can see things that
are coming from other components.

181
00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:31.810
So for example, what were the most
important factors that contributed

182
00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:33.670
to decreases in revenue?
So we see increased

183
00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:36.430
sales and marketing expenses,
different types of investments,

184
00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:37.970
different types of tax breaks.

185
00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.430
Essentially, each of these
augmented queries are

186
00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:42.650
providing us with
a slightly different set of results.

187
00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.030
And let's visualize that. What
did we actually get

188
00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:48.150
in geometric space in
response to these results?

189
00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:51.090
So again, we'll take our original
query embedding and

190
00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:53.430
our augmented query
embeddings and project them.

191
00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:56.170
And the next thing we'll
do is project our result embeddings.

192
00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:57.690
Before we do that,
we need to flatten the

193
00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:59.770
list because we have a
list of embeddings per query.

194
00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.710
We just want a
flat list of returned embeddings.

195
00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:03.370
And then we just
project them as before.

196
00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:05.790
And then let's
visualize what we get.

197
00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.439
And we see that using query expansion,
we were able to

198
00:08:08.439 --> 00:08:10.648
actually hit other related
parts of the data

199
00:08:10.648 --> 00:08:13.445
set that our single
original query may not have reached.

200
00:08:13.445 --> 00:08:15.555
And this gives us more
of a chance to find

201
00:08:15.555 --> 00:08:18.401
all of the related information,
especially in the context

202
00:08:18.401 --> 00:08:20.560
of more complex queries,
which require more

203
00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.818
and different types
of information to answer.

204
00:08:22.818 --> 00:08:25.419
So here we see that the
red X is our original query,

205
00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:28.559
the orange X's are the augmented, the
new queries generated for

206
00:08:28.559 --> 00:08:29.295
us by the LLM.

207
00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:31.455
And then once again,
the green circles

208
00:08:31.455 --> 00:08:34.056
represent the results
that we actually return by the

209
00:08:34.056 --> 00:08:37.343
retrieval system to the model.
One way to think about this is that

210
00:08:37.343 --> 00:08:39.355
a single query turns
into a single point

211
00:08:39.355 --> 00:08:41.269
in embedding space.
And a single point

212
00:08:41.269 --> 00:08:43.870
in embedding space likely doesn't
contain all of the

213
00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:45.980
information that you need
to answer a more

214
00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:49.612
complex query like this one. So
using this form of query expansion, where

215
00:08:49.612 --> 00:08:52.409
we generate multiple related
queries using an LLM, gives

216
00:08:52.409 --> 00:08:54.323
us a better chance
of capturing all of

217
00:08:54.323 --> 00:08:55.550
the related information.

218
00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:57.710
The downside of this, of course,
is now we have a lot

219
00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:01.050
more results than we had originally.
And we're not sure if and which

220
00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.850
of these results are actually
relevant to our query. In

221
00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:06.890
the next lab, using cross-encoder re-ranking,
we have a

222
00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:09.110
technique that allows us
to actually score the

